For a one-compartment model, give 1200 units into the depot every 12 hours at RATE=150 and F=0.8. Compare the depot amount at time zero after steady-state initialization with a transient schedule started 600 hours earlier. Both schedules give another dose at time zero.

```cpp
#include <stan/math.hpp>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  using std::vector;
  auto depot = [](double start, int additional, int steady) {
    return torsten::pmx_solve_onecpt(
        vector<double>{start, 0}, vector<double>{1200, 0},
        vector<double>{150, 0}, vector<double>{12, 0},
        vector<int>{1, 0}, vector<int>{1, 1},
        vector<int>{additional, 0}, vector<int>{steady, 0},
        vector<vector<double>>{{10, 80, 1.2}},
        vector<vector<double>>{{0.8, 1}}, vector<vector<double>>{{0, 0}})(0, 1);
  };
  std::cout << depot(0, 10, 1) << " " << depot(-600, 60, 0) << "\n";
}
```

On Metrum develop (`bb8266ea`), this prints `0.150748 0.822919`. The steady-state value should equal the long-run periodic value. Keeping the specified rate of 150 for 6.4 hours gives `0.150748` in both runs. The transient schedule instead delivers 120 units per hour for 8 hours.

The fix computes an infusion’s end time from `F * AMT / RATE` and preserves the specified rate during that interval, as the steady-state solver already does. Bioavailability then contributes to event-time derivatives. The regression compares repeated dosing with steady state across analytical and numerical solvers.

Add one independent response compartment with initial value zero and derivative 1 to a one-compartment PK model. A depot bolus at time zero is delayed by 0.35 hours. At one hour the response must equal 1 for every lag strictly between 0 and 1, so its derivative with respect to lag must be zero.

```cpp
#include <stan/math.hpp>
#include <iostream>

struct UnitSlope {
  template <class T, class Y, class PK, class P>
  auto operator()(const T&, const Y&, const PK&, const P&,
                  const std::vector<double>&, const std::vector<int>&,
                  std::ostream*) const {
    using S = stan::return_type_t<T, typename Y::Scalar, typename PK::Scalar,
                                  typename P::value_type>;
    return Eigen::Matrix<S, -1, 1>::Ones(1).eval();
  }
};

int main() {
  using stan::math::var;
  using std::vector;
  var lag = 0.35;
  auto y = torsten::pmx_solve_onecpt_rk45(
      UnitSlope{}, 1, vector<double>{0, 1}, vector<double>{100, 0},
      vector<double>{0, 0}, vector<double>{0, 0},
      vector<int>{1, 0}, vector<int>{1, 1}, vector<int>{0, 0}, vector<int>{0, 0},
      vector<vector<double>>{{10, 80, 1.2}}, vector<vector<double>>{{1, 1, 1}},
      vector<vector<var>>{{lag, 0, 0}});
  y(2, 1).grad();
  std::cout << y(2, 1).val() << " " << lag.adj() << "\n";
}
```

On Metrum develop (`bb8266ea`), this prints `1 1` (response and lag derivative). The expected output is `1 0`. The observation is strictly after the dose, away from the event-time discontinuity.

The coupled integrator takes the value of the step’s start time and loses its derivative after the lagged event. Integrating to the fixed start plus the differentiable step length `t1 - t0`, and measuring PK elapsed time from that same fixed start, preserves the derivative for this autonomous response. The same step routine serves the one- and two-compartment RK45 and BDF solvers.
